Rose: The sky is blue because of a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering, which happens when sunlight enters Earth's atmosphere and collides with gas molecules like nitrogen and oxygen. Blue light has a shorter wavelength than other colors in the visible spectrum, so it scatters much more easily off these tiny molecules compared to red or yellow light. All of this scattered blue light comes at you from every direction in the sky, which is why the entire sky appears blue to our eyes. At sunrise and sunset, sunlight has to travel through much more of the atmosphere at a lower angle, so most of the blue light gets scattered away before reaching you, leaving only the longer wavelength colors like orange and red visible on the horizon.
